# Bounded JSON HTTP transport

```sh
ddd recipe bounded-json-http --path <application> --dry-run
ddd recipe bounded-json-http --path <application>
```

This emits only the reusable transport and this handoff. It requires the native
Fetch, Headers, Web Streams and TextEncoder APIs supplied by Node 18+ or Bun. It
does not emit a Nest module, install packages, register routes, validate a human
identity or grant domain permissions. Import the client directly from
`src/shared/auth/oauth2-token-introspection/bounded-json-http.client.ts`.

The output path and unchanged template are shared with the OAuth recipes. A
matching existing file is preserved; a conflicting file, symlink or unsafe
destination rejects the complete plan before writes. Dry-run performs the same
preflight without creating files or directories. If later filesystem operations
fail, rollback preserves a concurrent owner's successor content and reports a
rollback conflict.

Supply a fixed, validated origin from deployment configuration. HTTPS is the
default; `trustedInternalHttp: true` is an explicit operator-owned exception for
a proven private HTTP service. Dynamic path segments are encoded individually.
Do not accept origins, authorization headers or arbitrary paths from callers.
Constructor headers are snapshotted and private. Request policies use bounded
deadlines and byte limits, refuse redirects and do not retry or positively cache
responses. Errors expose a safe category/status, never credentials or bodies.

`get`, `postForm`, `postJson`, `putJson` and `patchJson` return immutable JSON evidence. The
consumer must validate its exact schema and distinguish an authoritative denial
from an unavailable or malformed provider. A successful transport response does
not admit an account, link identities, complete a callback or authorize a write.
Provider-specific token and membership policy belongs to the owning application.
Use the owning application's tests to prove those decisions and verify this
mechanism under its supported runtime. Next.js consumers should add their own
`server-only` wrapper; the framework-neutral generated file has no Next import.
